export default "## What Happens to Discharge After Your Period Ends\n\nThe cervix produces fluid continuously, but the character of that fluid changes dramatically across the menstrual cycle. In the days immediately following your period, estrogen levels are low. The cervix produces little mucus, and what there is tends to be thick, white, or creamy. Some people experience almost no noticeable discharge for several days after their period ends, a dry phase.\n\nAs estrogen rises during the follicular phase, cervical mucus increases in volume and becomes progressively more fluid and clear. By ovulation, it has typically reached its peak: abundant, slippery, and stretchy, often described as resembling raw egg whites. This is the fertile-quality mucus that signals ovulation is near.\n\nAfter ovulation, progesterone takes over and cervical mucus thickens again, becoming less stretchy and more opaque, until the next period begins.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## What Normal Post-Period Discharge Looks Like\n\n### Days 1-3 After Period Ends\n\nDischarge is minimal or absent. Any discharge present is usually white, off-white, or slightly yellow when it dries on underwear. This color from drying is normal and does not indicate infection. Texture is sticky or tacky. Volume is low enough that many people do not notice it unless they look.\n\n### Days 4-7 After Period (or Until Ovulation Approaches)\n\nDischarge gradually increases in volume. It begins to appear creamier and white or cloudy. Some people describe it as lotion-like. This reflects rising estrogen beginning to stimulate cervical mucus production.\n\n### Pre-Ovulatory (Variable Timing)\n\nAs ovulation approaches, discharge becomes wetter, clearer, and more stretchy. At the most fertile point (typically a day or two before and on the day of ovulation), mucus is often transparent and can stretch between the fingers without breaking, a quality called Spinnbarkeit. After ovulation passes, it quickly returns to a thicker, cloudier texture.\n\n## When Discharge After a Period May Signal Something\n\n### Brown Discharge\n\nBrown discharge in the first day or two post-period is old blood clearing and is completely normal. Brown discharge appearing three or more days after your period ends, or appearing mid-cycle, is less typical. If it recurs across cycles, log it and bring it up with a provider.\n\n### Yellow or Green Discharge\n\nDistinctly yellow or green discharge (particularly if it has an unusual texture or smell) can indicate bacterial vaginosis or a sexually transmitted infection. Pale yellow discharge without odor or other symptoms is sometimes normal, but brighter yellow-green shades are not typical and warrant a provider visit.\n\n### Cottage-Cheese Texture\n\nThick, white discharge with a clumpy, cottage-cheese-like texture accompanied by itching or vulvar irritation is the characteristic presentation of a vaginal yeast infection. Yeast infections are common and treatable but are not self-resolving in most cases.\n\n### Fishy or Unusual Odor\n\nA persistent fishy odor, especially associated with thin, gray or white discharge, is the hallmark symptom of bacterial vaginosis. BV does not resolve on its own and requires antibiotic treatment.\n\n### Unusual Volume or Persistent Wetness\n\nOngoing unusually heavy discharge that is not related to the pre-ovulatory phase and is not accounted for by contraceptives or sexual arousal can sometimes reflect cervical ectropion or, less commonly, other cervical changes. Worth documenting if persistent.\n\n## What to Log\n\nTracking discharge takes less than a minute per day and builds a reference picture of your normal pattern:\n\n- **Volume:** none, minimal, moderate, or heavy\n- **Color:** clear, white, cream, yellow, brown, or other\n- **Texture:** sticky, creamy, watery, or stretchy\n- **Odor:** normal or unusual\n- **Cycle day:** always anchor discharge notes to where you are in your cycle\n- **Associated symptoms:** itching, burning, pelvic discomfort\n\nIf you notice a change that concerns you, a few days of logged data gives a provider much more to work with than a description from memory.\n\n## When to See a Healthcare Provider\n\n- Discharge has an unusual color (not white, cream, or clear) and is persistent\n- There is a noticeable, strong, or fishy odor\n- Discharge is accompanied by itching, burning, or irritation\n- You have pelvic pain or discomfort alongside discharge changes\n- Brown discharge persists beyond two days post-period\n\n## A Note on Discharge Data and Privacy\n\nDischarge patterns, logged day by day across a cycle, reveal fertility status, probable ovulation timing, and sometimes early pregnancy signals. This information is valuable for your health and also sensitive in a broader context.\n\nFlo Health was subject to an FTC enforcement action in 2021 for sharing period and health data (including fertility-related data) with Facebook and Google. A $59.5 million class action settlement followed in 2025. Period tracking data is not covered by HIPAA. In states where abortion is criminalized, detailed cycle data stored on company servers can be subpoenaed.\n\nOn-device trackers like Floriva, Drip, and Euki keep your discharge logs on your device only. The company never holds the data, so there is nothing to compel, breach, or sell.";
